@section What is the GPL?

A detailed description of the aims of the GPL and LGPL can be found on the @uref{http://www.gnu.org,GNU.org} web pages. The reasons why this license was selected for VW are given below.

A copy of the LPGL license can be found in appendix @xref{LGPL}.

The LGPL (Lesser General Public License) stipulates that this library is freely available, and can be modified and redistributed freely, providing all copyright and license statements are preserved. This means that anyone can use the library, but only the copyright holders (Oxford University) can change the licensing terms or release a commercial version of the code.

Unlike the more restrive GPL license, the LGPL states that although the library must always be free and retain all licensing and copyright statements, it can be used to build commercial applications under any license - ie. the fact that the VW libraries are LGPL has no impact on user applications. 

@section Why use the LGPL for VW?

Our choice of the LGPL for VW code is motivated by the need for us (and collaborating organisations/companies) to retain the ability to commercialise applications in the future. Releasing VW under the GPL would prevent this, since all code linking against a GPL library must also be GPL (and this is the main difference between GPL and LGPL).

VW could have been released under a completely closed license,
preventing 3rd parties releasing any code without the permission of Oxford
University. However, there is little to be gained by this. Most of the
algorithms in VW are not novel in any way, and other code to achieve
such tasks is freely available elsewhere. For computer vision tasks, the
novelty is in the user applications, not the underlying image and
feature classes. Whilst the applications may have commercial value, the
core libraries do not, and the LGPL neatly encapsulates this distinction
--- the library is free, but the applications might not be. 


@section What does this mean for the user?

Use of the LGPL has some ramifications for end-users, summarised below.

@subsection things you can do:

@itemize @bullet
   @item Write free (GPL, LGPL or any other license) applications that link against VW.
   @item Write (and sell) commercial applications which link against VW.
   @item Redistribute VW, providing all license and copyright statements are preserved.
   @item Change VW, providing your changes are also made freely available under the terms of the LGPL.
@end itemize

@subsection things you cannot do:

@itemize @bullet
   @item  Sell VW. A charge may be made for media or distribution costs, but not for the software itself.
   @item Distribute only a binary (compiled) version. Whenever VW is distributed, the source-code must also be made freely available (including any changes you have made). This need not be on the same media as the binary copy. 
@end itemize

Detailed descriptions of what can and cannot be done with LGPL'ed code
can be found in the license itself (@pxref{LGPL}). 
